<!-- MARKET OVERVIEW -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div>
      <div class="eyebrow">// Industry Structure</div>
      <h2 class="sh">The Global Spacesuit Market</h2>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="editorial" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;border:none;padding:0">
    <div class="editorial-main" style="border:none;padding:0;max-width:800px">
      <p class="edit-body">The global spacesuit market is structured around two sovereign government programs and an expanding commercial layer. NASA operates the legacy Enhanced EMU fleet for ISS orbital EVA, while Roscosmos maintains the Sokol KV-2 IVA rescue suit and Orlan-MK EVA fleet for the Russian ISS segment. China's CMSA operates independently with its Feitian Gen-2 EVA suit aboard Tiangong and is developing the Wangyu lunar suit targeting a crewed lunar landing before 2030.</p>
      <p class="edit-body">Three active commercial entrants have entered the US market. <strong>Axiom Space</strong> holds the NASA contract for the AxEMU lunar surface suit supporting Artemis III. <strong>Collins Aerospace</strong> is an active commercial development entrant. <strong>SpaceX</strong> operates a proprietary in-house IVA suit for Crew Dragon under NASA's Commercial Crew Program. ESA remains dependent on partner suits with no independent EVA capability.</p>
      <p class="edit-body">Market demand is primarily driven by three overlapping forces: Artemis lunar program procurement creating the first new US EVA suit contract in decades; ISS sustainment risk as the Enhanced EMU fleet ages past its design life — OIG 2025 flagged EMU sustainment as a critical program risk; and commercial space station development by Axiom Space, Starlab and Orbital Reef, each requiring independent suit solutions. The combined effect is the largest spacesuit procurement cycle since the Shuttle EMU in the 1980s.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

