  <div class="editorial" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;border:none;padding:0">
    <div class="editorial-main" style="border:none;padding:0;max-width:800px">

      <h3 class="fp-program" style="margin-top:0">What is the US spacesuit market?</h3>
      <p class="edit-body">The US spacesuit market is divided between NASA government procurement and commercial development. NASA's primary active EVA suit is the Enhanced EMU for ISS operations, now on critical sustainment watch per OIG 2025. The Artemis lunar program has contracted Axiom Space to develop the AxEMU commercial lunar suit for the Artemis III south-pole mission. SpaceX and Boeing both operate proprietary IVA suits for their Commercial Crew vehicles.</p>

      <h3 class="fp-program">How much does a spacesuit cost?</h3>
      <p class="edit-body">NASA's Enhanced EMU cost approximately $12 million per unit at peak production. China's Feitian Gen-2 suit has been reported at approximately $4.14 million per unit. Commercial suit development costs are not publicly disclosed by SpaceX or Axiom Space. The Apollo A7L program cost approximately $670 million in today's dollars for full development and production.</p>

      <h3 class="fp-program">Who manufactures spacesuits?</h3>
      <p class="edit-body">Active spacesuit manufacturers include ILC Dover (EMU suits for NASA), Axiom Space (AxEMU lunar suit), SpaceX (in-house IVA suit), David Clark Company (Boeing Starliner suit), NPP Zvezda (Sokol and Orlan suits for Roscosmos), and CASC (Feitian and Wangyu suits for CMSA/China). Collins Aerospace is an active commercial development entrant.</p>

      <h3 class="fp-program">What were Soviet spacesuits made of?</h3>
      <p class="edit-body">Soviet spacesuits used a combination of materials depending on generation. The SK-1 Vostok suit used rubberized fabric for the pressure bladder with a nylon restraint layer. The Orlan EVA suit family uses a semi-rigid fiberglass hard upper torso with soft fabric lower body constructed from multiple layers including a pressure bladder, restraint layer, thermal insulation and outer protective fabric. The Berkut EVA suit worn by Alexei Leonov used a double-bladder soft construction with rubberized inner and outer layers.</p>

      <h3 class="fp-program">What is the next generation US spacesuit?</h3>
      <p class="edit-body">The AxEMU developed by Axiom Space under NASA contract is the next generation US spacesuit, designed for lunar surface EVA on the Artemis III mission targeting the south pole of the Moon. It replaces the xEMU program which was cancelled in 2022 after mass overrun issues. NASA also continues to operate the Enhanced EMU for ISS orbital EVA.</p>

      <h3 class="fp-program">How long does a spacesuit last?</h3>
      <p class="edit-body">NASA's Enhanced EMU suits were designed for a service life of approximately 15 years but have been maintained in service significantly beyond that. China's Feitian Gen-2 suits are rated for 15 EVAs over 3 years, recently upgraded to 20 EVAs over 4 years for the D/E series. The Soviet Orlan suits have demonstrated operational lives of 4–7 years through station-based refurbishment programs.</p>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

